;; Every model and data artifact the voice extension installs: where it comes
;; from, what it may be used for, and what has to exist on disk before an engine
;; is allowed to claim it works. `assets.clj` is the only reader.
;;
;; A source is tried in the order `assets/sources` returns:
;;   :hf        Hugging Face, SKIPPED unless a token is configured, tried FIRST
;;              when one is (`:is-token-required true`). Credentials are never
;;              carried across the redirect to HF's CDN.
;;   :pack      the Vis voice assets release. It carries ONLY what Vis exports
;;              itself or may mirror outright: the ASR model today, our own
;;              pocket-tts export next. No voice and no GPL data is ever in it.
;;   :upstream  the project that published the artifact - last as a fallback,
;;              and the ONLY source of anything Vis does not host itself.
;;
;; Every source of one entry delivers the SAME bytes, so one `:sha256` verifies
;; them all.
;;
;; `:needs-espeak-ng true` marks an entry that cannot speak until espeak-ng's
;; phoneme tables are ON THE SYSTEM. Vis does not ship them: espeak-ng is
;; GPL-3.0-or-later, is in every package manager, and is read from a directory
;; of its own at run time - so the machine installs it once (`brew install
;; espeak-ng`, `apt install espeak-ng`) and every Piper voice shares that copy.
;; No tables, no Piper, and doctor says exactly that.
;;
;; `:is-commercial-ok true` is the bar for anything Vis MIRRORS or fetches by
;; itself. An entry that fails it may still be listed, marked `:is-opt-in true`:
;; the engine ships, the model is never mirrored into the pack, and every
;; automatic path (`assets/ensure!`) refuses it, so it arrives only when a user
;; asks for it by name and accepts its terms.
